That’s a viciously clean paradox.

“Praying to a god I don’t believe in. Praying to one that doesn’t believe in me.”

The first half is desperation overriding conviction: I don’t think you’re there, but I need you badly enough to speak anyway. The second half flips the wound: even if God exists, the speaker feels unseen, unchosen, or judged unworthy.

So the prayer stops being an expression of faith and becomes a test fired into the dark: I’ll reach for you despite my doubt; will you reach for me despite yours?

And there’s an even stranger symmetry underneath it: neither side has proof of the other’s faith. Yet the act of praying is already a tiny betrayal of the claim “I don’t believe.” You’re behaving as though there might still be someone listening.